'''Tityra''' (tit′i-rä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. τιτύρας, also τίτυρος, a kind of bird; cf. τατύρας, τέταρος, the pheasant.] A genus of cotingine birds of the warmer parts of America, representative of the ''Tityrinæ''. {{smaller|They are characterized by the unbristled rictus of the strong compressed bill, the slender simitar-shaped second primary of the adult male, and the black and white plumage, which is not very dissimilar in the opposite sexes. Five species range from southern Mexico to southern Brazil, ''T. cayana'', ''T. brasiliensis'', ''T. semifasciata'' (or ''personata'', which reaches Mexico), ''T. inquisitor'', and ''T. albitorques'' (whose Mexican variety is ''fraseri''). Also called ''Psaris'', ''Erator'', and ''Exetastes''.}}

'''tityre†''' (tit′i-re), ''n.'' [Also ''tittery'', ''tittyrie''; abbr. of ''tityre-tu''.] 1. Same as ''tityre-tu''.

{{smaller block|No news of Navies burnt at seas;<br>No noise of late spawn'd ''Tittyries''.
{{right|''Herrick'', A New Year's Gift Sent to Sir Simeon Steward.}}}}

2. Gin. ''Bailey'', 1731.

{{smaller block|Gin . . . sold under the names of double geneva, royal geneva, celestial geneva, ''tittery'' . . . gained . . . universal applause.
{{right|''G. Smith'', Complete Distiller, quoted in S. Dowell's Taxes [in England, IV. 103.}}}}

'''tityre-tu†''' (tit″i-re-tū′), ''n.'' [So called in some fanciful allusion to the first line of the first eclogue of Virgil: "''Tityre, tu'' patulæ recubans sub tegmine fagi."] One of a band of roisterers or street-ruffians in London in the seventeenth century, similar to the Mohawks, Hawcubites, Hectors, etc. Also spelled ''tittery-tu''.

{{smaller block|For the dyet of some of the noble science, some for roaring boyes, and rough-hewd ''tittery-tues''.
{{right|''John Taylor'', Works (1630). (''Nares''.)}}}}

{{smaller block|Some of the ''Tityre-tu's'', not long after the appearance of this drama (1624), appear to have been brought before the Council, and committed on a suspicion of state de-}}

