'''to'''<sup>1</sup> (tö), ''prep.'', ''adv.'', and ''conj.'' [< ME. ''to'', < AS. ''tō'' = OS. ''tō'', ''te'' = OFries. ''tō'', ''te'', ''ti'' = MD. D. ''toe'' = MLG. ''tō'', ''tū'', ''tē'', LG. ''to'' = OHG. ''zuo'', ''zua'', ''zō'', MHG. ''zuo'', ''zu'', G. ''zu'', to; not in Scand., where ''til'' is used (see ''till''<sup>2</sup>), or in Goth., where ''du'' is used (the supposed connection of ''du'' and ''to'' is not made out); = OIr. ''do'' = W. ''di'', later ''ddi'', W. ''i'', as a prefix ''dy-'' = Corn. ''dhi'', to; cf. Lith. ''da-'', = L. ''-do'' = Gr. -δε = Zend ''-da'', a demonstrative formative.] '''I.''' ''prep.'' A word used to express the relation of direction or tendency, with many modified and related senses. '''1.''' In the direction of; unto; toward: indicating direction or motion toward a place, point, goal, state, condition, or position, or toward something to be done or to be treated: opposed to ''from''.

{{smaller block|From every shires ende
Of Engelond ''to'' Caunterbury they wende.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 16.}}}}

{{smaller block|Be-hold [look] ''to'' th<sup>i</sup> souereyn in th<sup>e</sup> face with they eyene.
{{right|''Booke of Precedence'' (E. E. T. S., extra ser.), i. 58.}}}}

{{smaller block|Adonis hied him ''to'' the chase.
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|Me longeth sore ''to'' Bernysdale,
I may not be therfro.
{{right|''Lytell Geste of Robyn Hode'' (Child's Ballads, V. 121).}}}}

{{smaller block|Thou shalt ''to'' the Mall with us.
{{right|''Congreve'', Way of the World, i. 9.}}}}

{{smaller block|The natural disposition ''to'' any particular art, science, profession, or trade is very much to be consulted in the care of youth.
{{right|''Steele'', Spectator, No. 157.}}}}

{{smaller block|The General has fallen ''to'' one side in his large chair, whose arms support him from falling to the floor.
{{right|''W. M. Baker'', New Timothy, p. 260.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''2.''' As far as: indicating a point or limit reached or to be reached in space, time, or degree; expressing extent of continuance, or proceeding, or degree of comprehension, or inclusion.

{{smaller block|The sun in his sercle set vnto rest,
And the day ouer-drogh ''to'' the derke night.
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 10735.}}}}

{{smaller block|This Tower is easily to be seene ''to'' Milan in a cleare day.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 137.}}}}

{{smaller block|That which most exasperated the Silures was a report of certaine words cast out by the Emperor, that he would root them out ''to'' the verie name.
{{right|''Milton'', Hist. Eng., ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|Sir Tomkyn, drawing his sword, swore he was hers ''to'' the last drop of his blood.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, xi.}}}}

{{smaller block|And ever James was bending low,
''To'' his white jennet's saddlebow.
{{right|''Scott'', L. of the L., v. 21.}}}}

{{smaller block|He might have cogitated ''to'' all eternity without arriving at a result.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Social Statics, p. 23.}}}}

'''3.''' For; unto: indicating an actual or supposed limit to movement or action, or denoting destination, design, purpose, or aim: as, the horse is broken ''to'' saddle or harness.

{{smaller block|The souldiar preparynge hym selfe ''to'' the fielde
Leaues not at home his sworde and his shielde.
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 339.}}}}

{{smaller block|Wherefore was I ''to'' this keen mockery born?
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., ii. 2. 123.}}}}

{{smaller block|They must be dieted, as horses ''to'' a race.
{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 196.}}}}

{{smaller block|But ''to'' nobler sights
Michael from Adam's eyes the film removed.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xi. 412.}}}}

{{smaller block|I shall give Tom an eddication an' put him ''to'' a business.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Mill on the Floss, i. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|He was born ''to'' a large fortune, and had married a lady of the house of Noailles.
{{right|''The Century'', XLI. 368.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|If the field is planted ''to'' some other crop, the young lice mature on the grass-roots.
{{right|''Amer. Nat.'', December, 1889, p. 1105.}}}}

'''4.''' Unto: indicating a result or effect produced; denoting a consequence or end: as, he was flattered ''to'' his ruin; it was reported ''to'' her shame.

{{smaller block|I shall laugh myself ''to'' death. ''Shak.'', Tempest, ii. 2. 158.}}

{{smaller block|If any man in Englande should goe aboughte . . . to examine yo<sup>r</sup>. life ''to'' yo<sup>r</sup>. utter undoinge.
{{right|Quoted in ''H. Hall's'' Society in Elizabethan Age, viii.}}}}

{{smaller block|I must not leave this fellow; I will torment him ''to'' madness. ''Beau. and Fl.'', Woman-Hater, ii. 1.}}

{{smaller block|The moment the master put his horse ''to'' speed, his troops scattered in all directions. ''Irving'', Granada, p. 94.}}

{{smaller block|Then unto them I turned me, and I spake,
And I began: "Thine agonies, Francesca,
Sad and compassionate ''to'' weeping make me."
{{right|''Longfellow'', tr. of Dante's Inferno, v. 117.}}}}

'''5.''' Upon; besides: denoting addition, contribution, or possession.

{{smaller block|His breath and beauty set
Gloss on the rose, smell ''to'' the violet.
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 936.}}}}

{{smaller block|I have a thousand faces to deceive,
And, ''to'' those, twice as many tongues to flatter.
{{right|''Fletcher and Shirley'', Night-Walker, i. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Wisdom he has, and ''to'' his wisdom courage,
Temper ''to'' that, and unto all success.
{{right|''Sir J. Denham'', The Sophy. (''Latham.'')}}}}

'''6.''' Upon; on: denoting contact, junction, or union.

{{smaller block|Lean ''to'' no poste whils that ye stande present
Byfore your lorde. ''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 4.}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|Let me infold thee,
And hold thee ''to'' my heart.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, i. 4. 32.}}}}

{{smaller block|Then doe they sew a long and black thong ''to'' that thick hide or skin.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 195.}}}}

{{smaller block|When all night long a cloud clings ''to'' the hill.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Geraint.}}}}

7. Compared with: denoting comparison, proportion, or measure. {{smaller|Hence it is used in a strictly limited sense in expressing ratios or proportions: as, three is ''to'' twelve as four is ''to'' sixteen.}}

{{smaller block|There is no music ''to'' a Christian's knell.
{{right|''Marlowe'', Jew of Malta, iv. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|No, there were no man on the earth ''to'' Thomas,
If I durst trust him.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Every Man in his Humour, iii. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Name you any one thing that your citizen's wife comes short of ''to'' your lady.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Westward Ho, i. 1.}}}}

8. Against; over against: denoting opposition, contrast, or antithesis: as, to wager three ''to'' one; they engaged hand ''to'' hand.

{{smaller block|He sets the lesse by the greater, or the greater ''to'' the lesse, the equall ''to'' his equall, and by such confronting of them together driues out the true ods that is betwixt them.
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 197.}}}}

{{smaller block|For now we see through a glass, darkly; but then face ''to'' face.
{{right|1 Cor. xiii. 12.}}}}

{{smaller block|My hat ''to'' a halfpenny, Pompey proves the best worthy.
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., v. 2. 563.}}}}

{{smaller block|Tho that they were nine ''to'' ane,
They caused [them] take the chace.
{{right|''Battle of Balrinnes'' (Child's Ballads, VII. 229).}}}}

{{smaller block|Why will you fight against so sweet a passion,
And steel your heart ''to'' such a world of charms?
{{right|''Addison'', Cato, i. 6.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
