{{smaller block|A sharp conflict, hand ''to'' hand and man ''to'' man, took place on the battlements.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 54.}}}}

9. In accordance, congruity, or harmony with: denoting agreement, adaptation, or adjustment: as, a plan drawn ''to'' scale; painted ''to'' the life.

{{smaller block|Ihesu, thou kan me sone amende;<br>Thou has me made ''to'' thi lyknes.
{{right|''Political Poems'', etc. (ed. Furnivall), p. 105.}}}}

{{smaller block|And whan ye knowe what it is, loke ye, performe it ''to'' his plesier.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), i. 58.}}}}

{{smaller block|His horses and his men<br>Suited in satin ''to'' their master's colours.
{{right|''Peele'', Polyhymnia (ed. Bullen).}}}}

{{smaller block|Fashion your demeanour ''to'' my looks.
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., ii. 2. 33.}}}}

{{smaller block|Now, Maria, here is a character ''to'' your taste.
{{right|''Sheridan'', School for Scandal, i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|It was a most difficult matter to keep the tunnel ''to'' grade.
{{right|''Sci. Amer.'', N. S., LXIV. 52.}}}}

10. In accompaniment with: as, she sang ''to'' his guitar.

{{smaller block|They move<br>In perfect phalanx ''to'' the Dorian mood<br>Of flutes and soft recorders.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 550.}}}}

{{smaller block|Let us but practise a while; and then you shall see me dance the whole Dance ''to'' the Violin.
{{right|''Wycherley'', Gentleman Dancing-Master, iv. 1.}}}}

11. In the character, quality, or shape of; for; as.

{{smaller block|And Floriz he maketh stonde upriȝt<br>And ther he dubbede him ''to'' kniȝt.
{{right|''King Horn'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 73.}}}}

{{smaller block|He badde me wite of yow what he shulde haue ''to'' rewarde.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), i. 72.}}}}

{{smaller block|He hath a pretty young man ''to'' his son, whose name is Civility.
{{right|''Bunyan'', Pilgrim's Progress, i.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
