{{smaller block|He took a morsel of early lamb ''to'' his dinner.
{{right|''Trollope'', Last Chronicle of Barset, xlix.}}}}

12. Regarding; concerning; as to: denoting relation: as, to plead ''to'' the charge; to speak ''to'' the question.

{{smaller block|Where we may leisurely
Each one demand and answer ''to'' his part
Perform'd in this wide gap of time.
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., v. 3. 153.}}}}

{{smaller block|It takes away my faith ''to'' anything
He shall hereafter speak.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Sejanus, iii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|At these meetings, any of the members of the churches may come, if they please, and speak their minds freely, in the fear of God, ''to'' any matter.
{{right|''Penn'', Rise and Progress of Quakers, iv.}}}}

{{smaller block|[Dr.] ''To'' a lady's lounging-chair . . . in ebonized wood....................................£ 16-16-0
''To'' a gentleman's Etruscan do. do., cabriole legs.................................... 17-17-0
{{right|''Miss Braddon'', Hostages of Fortune, p. 115.}}}}

13. Denoting application or attention: as, he fell ''to'' work.

{{smaller block|Sing me now asleep;
Then ''to'' your offices, and let me rest.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., ii. 2. 7.}}}}

{{smaller block|They begin with porridge, then they fall ''to'' capon, or so forth. ''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, i. 1.}}

{{smaller block|The bride and her party, having arrived at the bridegroom's house, sit down ''to'' a repast.
{{right|''E. W. Lane'', Modern Egyptians, I. 210.}}}}

14. In connection with; appurtenant: denoting attribution, appurtenance, or belonging: as, a cap with a tassel ''to'' it.
